import Native

-- What `swap` and `exchange` are refused for. Each function is one rule; the expectation beside
-- this file is the whole of what it reports.

data Handle {value: I64}

-- Both places are written, so both are exclusive for as long as the operation runs. `swap(x, x)` is
-- the smallest program that asks for one storage to be two of them, and it is rejected by the
-- exclusivity rule that was there before swap existed rather than by anything written for it. That
-- is the whole reason these take their places from `&` arguments.
fn selfSwap() -> I64:
    let &value = 1 :: I64
    swap(value, value)
    return value

-- An immutable binding names storage nothing may write, which is as true of an exchange as of an
-- assignment: what these do to a place is write it.
fn immutableSwap() -> I64:
    let x = 1 :: I64
    let &y = 2 :: I64
    swap(x, y)
    return x

fn immutableExchange() -> I64:
    let slot = Handle {value: 1}
    let old = exchange(slot, Handle {value: 2})
    return old.value

-- A subscript in `&` position used to be here, and is not a rejection any more: `swap(xs[i], xs[j])`
-- and `exchange(xs[i], v)` both compile. See test/lib/Exchange.yana, which runs them.

{-
   And what a subscript in `&` position *is* refused for, which is everything else.

   Two elements of one container are two exclusive borrows the index alone tells apart, and nothing
   in this analysis reads an index: `xs[i]` reaches the checker as a borrow of a raw pointer offset
   from a base loaded out of the container, and a raw pointer is what the borrow checker declines to
   reason about. Proving `i != j` is a value analysis rather than a borrow one.

   So the pair is refused wherever the operation could tell: even `xs[0]` twice, since the rule is
   about what the analysis can say and not about what this line happens to have written. `swap` and
   `exchange` are the exemption, and the two functions above it are why - the exemption is per
   borrow, so a borrow handed to anything else is checked however the other one is used.
-}
fn twice(&a: I64, &b: I64) -> {}:
    a = a + 1
    b = b + 10

fn distinctElements() -> {}:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3] :: [I64]
    twice(xs[0], xs[1])

fn sameElement() -> {}:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3] :: [I64]
    twice(xs[0], xs[0])

-- The accessor written out, which is what the subscript is sugar for and reaches the same rule.
fn throughTheAccessor() -> {}:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3] :: [I64]
    twice(getMut(xs, 0), getMut(xs, 1))

-- A fixed array, which reaches its element through a `Flat` descriptor built at the call - so the
-- two borrows are rooted in two different locals and are one container all the same. Local::viewOf
-- is the chain that says so.
fn fixedArray() -> {}:
    let &fa = [1, 2, 3] :: [I64 *3]
    twice(fa[0], fa[1])

-- Two containers are two containers: the root each borrow traces back to is what tells them apart,
-- and this is not a rejection.
fn separateContainers() -> {}:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3] :: [I64]
    let &ys = [4, 5, 6] :: [I64]
    twice(xs[0], ys[0])
